## LEASES & EXPIRY vs REVOCATION — dense findings

Everything below is sourced. **MEASURED** = a number someone actually observed; **DESIGNED/CLAIMED** = policy text, doctrine, or vendor assertion.

---

## 1. CA/Browser Forum Ballot SC-081v3 — the industry's formal convergence

**URL:** https://cabforum.org/2025/04/11/ballot-sc081v3-introduce-schedule-of-reducing-validity-and-data-reuse-periods/ (note: the URL is `sc081v3`, not `sc-081`; the `sc-081` slug 404s)

**Dates (exact, from the ballot page):**
- Ballot created in Server Certificate WG: **Tuesday, April 8, 2025** (per https://cabforum.org/ballots/, status "Ballot Passed - IPR Review Period Cleared")
- Discussion opened: **2025-01-28 17:00 UTC**; discussion closed on/after **2025-04-01 17:00 UTC**
- Voting: **2025-04-04 19:30 UTC → 2025-04-11 19:30 UTC**
- IPR Review Period: **2025-04-13 03:00 UTC → 2025-05-13 03:00 UTC**

**Vote tally (MEASURED):**
- **Certificate Issuers: 30 votes — 25 YES, 0 NO, 5 ABSTAIN.**
  - YES: Amazon, Asseco Data Systems (Certum), Buypass, Certigna (DHIMYOTIS), Certinomis, DigiCert, Disig, D-TRUST, eMudhra, Fastly, GlobalSign, GoDaddy, HARICA, iTrusChina, Izenpe, NAVER Cloud Trust Services, OISTE Foundation, Sectigo, SHECA, SSL.com, SwissSign, Telia, TrustAsia, VikingCloud, Visa
  - ABSTAIN: Entrust, IdenTrust, Japan Registry Services, SECOM Trust Systems, TWCA
- **Certificate Consumers: 4 votes — 4 YES (Apple, Google, Microsoft, Mozilla), 0 NO, 0 abstain.**
- Quorum was **16**; met. Proposed by **Clint Wilson (Apple)**, endorsed by Nick France (Sectigo), Ryan Dickson (Google Chrome), Ben Wilson (Mozilla).

**The schedule, verbatim from BR.md §6.3.2** (https://github.com/cabforum/servercert/blob/main/docs/BR.md):

| Issued on or after | Issued before | Max Validity Period |
|---|---|---|
| — | 2026-03-15 | **398 days** |
| 2026-03-15 | 2027-03-15 | **200 days** |
| 2027-03-15 | 2029-03-15 | **100 days** |
| 2029-03-15 | — | **47 days** |

(SHOULD NOT values are one day lower at each step: 397 / 199 / 99 / 46. "For the purpose of calculations, a day is measured as 86,400 seconds.")

**Data-reuse schedules, BR.md §4.2.1:**

*Domain Name and IP Address validation (DCV) reuse:*

| On or after | Before | Max reuse |
|---|---|---|
| — | 2026-03-15 | **398 days** |
| 2026-03-15 | 2027-03-15 | **200 days** |
| 2027-03-15 | 2029-03-15 | **100 days** |
| 2029-03-15 | — | **10 days** |

*Subject Identity Information validation reuse:* **825 days → 398 days** effective **2026-03-15**.

**The stated rationale — the argument that revocation does not work** (verbatim from the ballot's "Adjacent Solutions → Revocation" section):

> "Certificate status services, such as CRLs and OCSP, are technologies which do not adequately protect relying parties at the current scale of the internet, whether due to privacy concerns, performance impacts, timeliness of relevant statuses, the accuracy (and usability) of data, or other inadequacies. A reduction to certificate lifetimes provides firm protection to users, **independent of certificate status services**."

> "relying solely on revocation is untenable for a number of reasons" — broken into **Scalability** ("This growth can and does overwhelm existing infrastructure"), **Reliability** ("Not every potentially problematic certificate is revoked, let alone revoked in a timely manner. **Even when populations of certificates which must be revoked within a very specific timeframe are clearly identified, revocation does not consistently occur as expected.**" … "Certificate validity periods, and their enforcement by relying parties, are **incredibly reliable**."), and **User Impact** (dozens of status queries per page, timeouts/retries).

Footnote [6]: "Fundamentally, the above property of certificates is not something which can be addressed through revocation of certificates without much more substantial changes to the ecosystem." Cited evidence includes https://insecure.design/ and Ma et al., "stale certs" IMC 2023 (https://zanema.com/papers/imc23_stale_certs.pdf).

Prior art the ballot names: **Ballot 185** (2017-02-24, https://cabforum.org/2017/02/24/ballot-185-limiting-the-lifetime-of-certificates/) and **Ballot SC-22** (2019-09-10). Also SC-063 introduced a **Short-Lived Subscriber Certificate** class at **10 days, reducing to 7**.

