## 11. OAuth / token-TTL failures in production

### (a) Okta support-system breach, Oct 2023
- **Okta root-cause post (2023-11-03):** https://sec.okta.com/articles/2023/11/unauthorized-access-oktas-support-case-management-system-root-cause/
  - **Window of unauthorized access: September 28 – October 17, 2023.**
  - **Root cause, verbatim:** "an employee had signed-in to their personal Google profile on the Chrome browser of their Okta-managed laptop. **The username and password of the service account had been saved into the employee's personal Google account.**"
  - **134 customers affected (<1% of Okta's customer base); 5 customers experienced actual session hijacking.**
  - HAR files uploaded to support cases **contained live session tokens**, usable to hijack sessions.
  - Detection timeline: **Sept 29** 1Password reports suspicious activity; **Oct 2** BeyondTrust reports; **Oct 13** BeyondTrust supplies an attacker IP as IoC; **Oct 16** Okta correlates the IP to service-account activity; **Oct 17** account disabled, file access confirmed; **Oct 19** additional downloads found and all affected customers identified.
  - **The detection gap is the lease-vs-audit lesson: Okta missed the downloads for 14 days** because different log event types were generated depending on the access method used.
- **Okta initial disclosure (2023-10-20):** https://sec.okta.com/articles/2023/10/tracking-unauthorized-access-oktas-support-system/ — describes HAR files as containing "sensitive data, including cookies and session tokens, that malicious actors can use to impersonate valid users"; Okta's mitigation was **"revocation of embedded session tokens"** — an explicit fallback to revocation because the tokens' own TTL was too long to save them. Named customers do not appear in Okta's own posts; 1Password and BeyondTrust appear in the November RCA timeline.
- Closure post: https://sec.okta.com/harfiles — Stroz Friedberg forensic investigation found "no evidence of further malicious activity beyond what was previously determined." Remediations announced: zero standing privileges for admins, MFA for protected actions, **IP binding to prevent session takeover**, network-zone restrictions on API access. (IP binding is a *first-party caveat* in macaroon terms — contextual confinement added because expiry alone failed.)
- Secondary press for the 134 figure: https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/okta-breach-134-customers-exposed-in-october-support-system-hack/ (2023-11-03).

### (b) Cloudflare Thanksgiving 2023 — the missed rotation
**URL:** https://blog.cloudflare.com/thanksgiving-2023-security-incident/

- Timeline: **Okta compromise Oct 18, 2023** → **Nov 14–17** threat-actor recon and initial access → **Nov 22** persistent access established → **Nov 23** detected, response begins → **Nov 24** all access terminated → **Jan 5** "Code Red" remediation concluded.
- **The number that matters: of thousands of credentials rotated after the Okta breach, exactly 4 were missed** — "mistakenly believed to be unused":
  1. a **Moveworks service token** (remote access to Atlassian),
  2. a **Smartsheet service account** (administrative access to Jira),
  3. a **Bitbucket service account** (source code),
  4. an **AWS environment credential** (marketplace apps).
- Blast radius (MEASURED): **36 Jira tickets accessed out of 2,059,357**; **202 wiki pages of 194,100**; **76 source repositories of 11,904** downloaded (network config, backups, infra management); **Sliver C2** installed for persistence. Attacker search terms included "remote access", "secret", "token".
- Remediation scale: **>5,000 production credentials rotated**, **4,893 systems forensically triaged**, every machine on the global network reimaged and rebooted.
- The structural lesson is exact: **revocation-by-rotation is a manual enumeration, and enumeration misses. 4 out of ~5,000 (~0.08%) was enough.** A short lease would have expired all four without anyone having to remember them.

### (c) Microsoft Storm-0558 — a signing key with no working expiry
- **Microsoft's incident analysis (2023-07-14):** https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2023/07/14/analysis-of-storm-0558-techniques-for-unauthorized-email-access/
  - **~25 organizations** affected, including government agencies plus related consumer accounts.
  - Activity began **May 15, 2023**; discovered **June 16, 2023** (by a customer — the US State Department — not by Microsoft).
  - An **inactive MSA consumer signing key** was used to forge tokens. A validation flaw let a *consumer* MSA key be accepted for *Azure AD enterprise* tokens.
  - **Mitigation dates:** **June 26** OWA stopped accepting tokens from the affected API; **June 27** tokens signed with the acquired key blocked; **June 29 all MSA signing keys valid at the time of the incident were revoked**; **July 3** key usage blocked for impacted consumer customers.
- **Microsoft's September 2023 technical post-mortem** (https://msrc.microsoft.com/blog/2023/09/results-of-major-technical-investigations-for-storm-0558-key-acquisition/) now **301-redirects to the MSRC blog index — original text NOT REACHED directly.** Content recovered via Wiz's analysis, https://www.wiz.io/blog/key-takeaways-from-microsofts-latest-storm-0558-report:
  - The key was captured in **a crash dump from a crash that occurred in April 2021**; the dump moved from the isolated production network to the corporate debugging environment.
  - **The key was issued April 2016 and "expired in April 2021, but remained valid until it was finally rotated in July 2023."** That is the single most load-bearing fact in this whole topic: **a nominally expired key remained honored for ~2 years and 3 months because nothing enforced its expiry.** One key served for over seven years.
  - Storm-0558 plausibly held the key **"for over two years prior to being discovered in June 2023."** Microsoft could only "partially account for all of this threat actor's activity… between April 2021 and May 2023" due to log-retention limits.
  - Root validation defect: the Azure AD SDK "did not include functionality to properly validate an authentication token's issuer ID."
- **CSRB report:** released **April 2, 2024** (report dated March 20, 2024), announced at https://www.dhs.gov/news/2024/04/02/cyber-safety-review-board-releases-report-microsoft-online-exchange-incident-summer and https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/cyber-safety-review-board-releases-report-microsoft-online-exchange-incident-summer-2023. The Board drew on "20 organizations and experts." Its headline finding, as summarized on Wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_Safety_Review_Board): **"Microsoft's security culture was inadequate and requires an overhaul,"** and Microsoft "failed to detect the compromise of its cryptographic crown jewels on its own, relying instead on a customer." Victims included the US Department of State, Department of Commerce, and House of Representatives.
- **The full CSRB PDF is NOT REACHED.** `cisa.gov`, `dhs.gov`, and `media.defense.gov` all return **403** to both WebFetch and curl; a GitHub code search found no full-text mirror. **Therefore the specific figures "60,000 State Dept emails" and "automated key rotation paused since 2021" are NOT VERIFIED from primary source in this session** — do not cite them as CSRB findings without retrieving the PDF. What *is* verified from primary/secondary sources: ~25 organizations (Microsoft), and the key's April-2021-expiry-to-July-2023-rotation gap (Wiz, from Microsoft's September 2023 post).

---

## Cross-cutting observations (reporting only, not recommending)

- **The convergence is real and dated.** Four browser vendors and 25 CAs voted, unanimously among consumers, on **2025-04-11** to put certificate lifetime on a rail from 398 → **47 days by 2029-03-15**, and DCV reuse from 398 → **10 days**, with the ballot's own text naming revocation as structurally inadequate. Let's Encrypt independently turned off OCSP entirely (**2025-08-06**, after ~340 B requests/month) and shipped 160-hour certificates with **no revocation URLs at all** (**GA 2026-01-15**).
- **The empirical case rests on three measurements, all 2014–2015:** 4% of Alexa Top 1M revoked post-Heartbleed while 23% reissued and 14% of reissuers reused the compromised key (IMC 2014); >8% of served certs revoked, CRLSet covering **0.35%** of revocations, and **zero** mobile browsers checking anything (IMC 2015); and Langley's soft-fail argument that the check fails exactly when it is needed.
- **Nobody actually abandoned revocation — they demoted it to a rare backstop and made the lease the primary control.** SPIFFE keeps CRL fields in the Workload API while declaring WIT-SVID revocation propagation out of scope. Biscuit generates `revocation_id` facts but leaves the list to the host. Fly.io runs both a `ValidityWindow` caveat *and* revocation-by-nonce. Let's Encrypt still publishes CRLs. Kerberos's only revocation (double krbtgt reset) is itself **gated on waiting one full TTL**.
- **The three 2023 incidents are all failures of the *revocation* path, not the expiry path:** Okta had to revoke session tokens whose TTL outlived the breach; Cloudflare's manual rotation enumeration missed 4 of ~5,000 credentials; and Microsoft's MSA key sat *nominally expired* yet honored for 27 months. In each case a short, automatically-enforced lease would have closed the window without anyone having to notice, list, or act.
